WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.007
Back in the storeroom.

2
00:00:04.007 --> 00:00:06.857
You've met two machines that each picked a side.

3
00:00:06.857 --> 00:00:10.243
The dispenser only hands fruit out, so it's declared out.

4
00:00:10.243 --> 00:00:13.628
The compost bin only takes fruit in, so it's declared in.

5
00:00:13.833 --> 00:00:17.848
But most of the storeroom is crates, and crates are the busy kind.

6
00:00:17.848 --> 00:00:20.950
All day, helpers take apples out and put apples in.

7
00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:27.763
That's why, two lessons ago, a crate of apples couldn't pass as a crate of fruit: someone might drop a lemon in.

8
00:00:28.667 --> 00:00:31.334
In code, MutableList is that busy crate.

9
00:00:31.334 --> 00:00:35.870
It has get, which hands an element out, and add, which takes one in.

10
00:00:35.870 --> 00:00:38.271
Array is the same, with get and set.

11
00:00:38.271 --> 00:00:42.005
T sits on both sides, so the type has to stay invariant.

12
00:00:42.933 --> 00:00:48.189
Today, one helper has a single job: copy the apples from a crate into the shop window.

13
00:00:48.189 --> 00:00:50.816
They'll only take apples out of that crate.

14
00:00:50.816 --> 00:00:56.316
So the clerk at the door gives an instruction: for this job, treat the crate as take-only.

15
00:00:56.316 --> 00:00:59.493
The helper, who has heard all about the lemon, nods.

16
00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:01.456
The crate itself didn't change.

17
00:01:01.456 --> 00:01:05.196
The next helper through the door may hear a different instruction.

18
00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:09.182
So here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

19
00:01:09.182 --> 00:01:11.572
My function only reads from the crate.

20
00:01:11.572 --> 00:01:13.333
Why won't it take my apples?

21
00:01:13.333 --> 00:01:17.924
Do I really have to copy them into a new crate of fruit, just to call it?

22
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:22.323
Here's the job in Kotlin, with no variance at all.

23
00:01:22.323 --> 00:01:27.639
copyRow takes two arrays of Fruit, and copies each item from one into the other.

24
00:01:27.639 --> 00:01:30.696
The apples are an array of two: Gala and Fuji.

25
00:01:30.696 --> 00:01:34.152
The window is an Array&lt;Fruit&gt; that holds two lemons.

26
00:01:34.367 --> 00:01:37.760
Call it, and Kotlin says: argument type mismatch.

27
00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:41.847
Actual type is Array&lt;Apple&gt;, but Array&lt;Fruit&gt; was expected.

28
00:01:42.067 --> 00:01:43.931
Let's be fair to the compiler.

29
00:01:43.931 --> 00:01:48.529
Seen as an array of fruit, the apples could get a lemon written into them.

30
00:01:48.529 --> 00:01:51.387
That's the lemon in the crate, all over again.

31
00:01:51.387 --> 00:01:52.692
But look at the body.

32
00:01:52.692 --> 00:01:54.183
It never writes to from.

33
00:01:54.183 --> 00:01:55.675
It only takes items out.

34
00:01:56.600 --> 00:02:01.655
The usual way out is to copy the apples into a fresh array of fruit, or to cast.

35
00:02:01.655 --> 00:02:06.836
Copying a whole crate just so someone can look inside feels like a lot of lifting.

36
00:02:06.836 --> 00:02:11.639
Wouldn't it be nice to tell the compiler, for this one parameter: take-only?

37
00:02:12.567 --> 00:02:13.073
You can.

38
00:02:13.073 --> 00:02:18.012
Write out in front of the type, right where it's used: from, Array&lt;out Fruit&gt;.

39
00:02:18.012 --> 00:02:20.544
That's the clerk's instruction, in code.

40
00:02:20.767 --> 00:02:22.119
Same call as before.

41
00:02:22.119 --> 00:02:25.635
Does it compile now, and what ends up in the window?

42
00:02:25.635 --> 00:02:26.717
Pause and guess.

43
00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:31.194
It compiles.

44
00:02:31.194 --> 00:02:33.878
The demo prints: window, Gala, Fuji.

45
00:02:33.878 --> 00:02:37.381
The two lemons were replaced by the two apples.

46
00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:39.100
Let's map it.

47
00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:42.239
The crate is the Array type, and it doesn't change.

48
00:02:42.239 --> 00:02:44.454
The helper is the function, copyRow.

49
00:02:44.454 --> 00:02:48.208
The clerk's instruction is the out, written on one parameter.

50
00:02:48.208 --> 00:02:52.947
And the next helper is the next function, which may ask for a different view.

51
00:02:53.867 --> 00:02:57.531
Through that view, get still works, and gives you a Fruit.

52
00:02:57.531 --> 00:02:58.541
But set is gone.

53
00:02:58.541 --> 00:03:07.007
Try to put a lemon into from, and Kotlin says: receiver type Array&lt;out Fruit&gt; contains out projection, which prohibits the use of set.

54
00:03:07.933 --> 00:03:08.875
Now the names.

55
00:03:08.875 --> 00:03:18.027
An out or an in written on one use of a type is a type projection: not a change to Array, just a narrower view of it, for one parameter.

56
00:03:18.027 --> 00:03:22.737
And writing variance where a type is used is called use-site variance.

57
00:03:23.667 --> 00:03:29.673
Java's wildcards, question mark extends and question mark super, are the same idea.

58
00:03:29.673 --> 00:03:32.061
But Java arrays are the odd case.

59
00:03:32.061 --> 00:03:38.357
Here's copyRow in Java, with plain Fruit arrays, copying the apples into a Lemon array.

60
00:03:38.357 --> 00:03:39.515
Does it compile?

61
00:03:39.515 --> 00:03:40.383
Does it run?

62
00:03:40.383 --> 00:03:41.541
Pause and guess.

63
00:03:44.533 --> 00:03:45.381
It compiles.

64
00:03:45.381 --> 00:03:53.927
Every Java array has been covariant since Java 1.0, so an Apple array passes as a Fruit array, and so does a Lemon array.

65
00:03:53.927 --> 00:04:01.343
Then it runs, and the JVM, the machine that runs the code, checks each store: ArrayStoreException, Apple.

66
00:04:02.267 --> 00:04:05.364
Java arrays never got the clerk's instruction.

67
00:04:05.364 --> 00:04:11.155
They got a guard on the shelf, who shouts after the apple is already in the wrong box.

68
00:04:11.155 --> 00:04:13.647
Kotlin's clerk stops you at the door.

69
00:04:13.647 --> 00:04:20.448
The same call in Kotlin doesn't compile: actual type is Array&lt;Lemon?&gt;, but Array&lt;Fruit&gt; was expected.

70
00:04:21.367 --> 00:04:23.102
And the instruction is free.

71
00:04:23.102 --> 00:04:29.423
Array&lt;out Fruit&gt; compiles to a plain Fruit array, so all the checking happens before the program runs.

72
00:04:30.333 --> 00:04:33.256
So one invariant type can be seen three ways.

73
00:04:33.256 --> 00:04:37.283
The plain view takes out and puts in, for the exact type only.

74
00:04:37.283 --> 00:04:39.101
The out view only takes out.

75
00:04:39.101 --> 00:04:42.413
And the in view only puts in, like the compost bin.

76
00:04:43.333 --> 00:04:45.589
Here the clerk picture breaks a little.

77
00:04:45.589 --> 00:04:49.290
A helper told take-only wouldn't tip the crate out on the floor.

78
00:04:49.290 --> 00:04:52.991
But a take-only view of a MutableList still lets you call clear.

79
00:04:52.991 --> 00:04:55.304
A projection limits which types flow in.

80
00:04:55.304 --> 00:04:57.617
It doesn't stop the crate from changing.

81
00:04:58.533 --> 00:04:59.727
And the guardrail.

82
00:04:59.727 --> 00:05:02.844
Project only types that have to stay invariant.

83
00:05:02.844 --> 00:05:10.671
Kotlin's read-only List is already declared out, so List&lt;out Fruit&gt; just earns a warning: the projection is redundant.

84
00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:16.618
So, do you have to copy your apples to call a function that only reads?

85
00:05:16.618 --> 00:05:16.830
No.

86
00:05:16.830 --> 00:05:18.315
The crate stays busy.

87
00:05:18.315 --> 00:05:21.920
You just tell one function, for one job: take-only.

88
00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:23.687
That's a type projection.

89
00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:24.685
Quick check.

90
00:05:24.685 --> 00:05:28.999
A function only puts apples into a crate, and never reads from it.

91
00:05:28.999 --> 00:05:31.941
Which word goes on its parameter: out, or in?

92
00:05:35.133 --> 00:05:35.358
In.

93
00:05:35.358 --> 00:05:38.349
MutableList&lt;in Apple&gt;, a drop-only view.

94
00:05:38.349 --> 00:05:41.041
That's the next part of this lesson.
